#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e40a26-493d-5b63-b2a7-b0c0857a63c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y-z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153} {:answer/id #uuid "96600223-6ac2-5aae-af5f-d339a3bf95ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3y-z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd52b89-44ba-5ac6-aa3e-ee8390f035fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-4y-z+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317156} {:answer/id #uuid "83c33c72-a071-596b-90b3-cb3ce07d269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-4z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} :answer-field/id #uuid "39431669-f8b7-5624-822e-3855a0a51429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ce23ca-0088-57fb-b4ca-21f59a96ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-127967" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i+4j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd320510-1075-5e34-8eeb-c578ca849a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188} {:answer/id #uuid "6489f1d6-5bad-54b1-8041-e0b0f9e9e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189} {:answer/id #uuid "7d72cb60-ea74-5c35-8b5a-0618e41e1504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b80d53-2e9a-538d-9b63-efb1fb02a239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z-26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317191} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3cc707-a5f8-5ce6-958c-76852cfc90f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc6b7572-6525-5f0a-acdf-da49eecac498" :question/math-academy-id "q-330498" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55723dcb-605f-595d-8fae-1ee5436e7a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384648} {:answer/id #uuid "42a8ae72-16c3-587a-a3e7-b75bffe928ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5y+z-70=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384649} {:answer/id #uuid "803a1755-2134-5aa8-a86a-8015bacc22d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+y-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384650} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5cb89-4f60-5887-afe3-a7c5a3523298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z+70=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384651} {:answer/id #uuid "31dcdae4-9b1f-53d5-a9d2-7fbe89fc944a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y+3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55723dcb-605f-595d-8fae-1ee5436e7a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15x-8y-3z-67=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384648} :answer-field/id #uuid "362de8bd-fdfa-5aa6-b00e-5111d888298e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5938084-81eb-59aa-b8c4-275f8497be94" :question/math-academy-id "q-33142" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951c6a2b-706d-5fc0-a1db-31a994bfc62e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117541} {:answer/id #uuid "b162df82-2366-5bd5-a612-c1ed54ffbc67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x + y + 2z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117542} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4541cf-b761-55dd-8dce-1934399f0089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117543} {:answer/id #uuid "43586768-6810-5b8c-b59a-7cf0eb1247ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x + y + 2z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117544} {:answer/id #uuid "75b979ae-630b-5445-b684-42e7a983e382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b979ae-630b-5445-b684-42e7a983e382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 3y - z - 42 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117545} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e271cb-b49f-584f-8191-ea123e7adc06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117540}] :question/id #uuid "b0dec6df-1301-5550-8bf9-7363cae85702" :question/math-academy-id "q-33165" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8, 3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n\n$\\frac{x + 2}{4} = \\frac{y - 1}{3} = \\frac{z - 2}{-1}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0eb7ad-9b50-5dc6-8b87-7f76312128ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0eb7ad-9b50-5dc6-8b87-7f76312128ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384670} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27f8f14-201a-514c-be9b-ed56526f88d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32d4500-7940-5053-86bb-fbf9704e32fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32d4500-7940-5053-86bb-fbf9704e32fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384672} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f9f4ed-dcee-5879-891b-4aeea776472c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8a3a99-7496-5337-b5d9-580ecd2bdac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8a3a99-7496-5337-b5d9-580ecd2bdac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384674} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ce9af2-3886-50d4-8ccd-8959a18d4900" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e72d0f67-c2b4-5692-91cc-01e26d063987" :question/math-academy-id "q-336228" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5x+2y+z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$\n{{field-2}} $,\\,2\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y+z-4 & =0 \\\\ z & =-5x-2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,\\,2\\,,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d0d219-ec4f-53bb-97f0-b1c5bd529520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675} :answer-field/id #uuid "842469ef-6b3f-52d4-b990-df6cd1e2c154" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677} :answer-field/id #uuid "c362e1f6-816b-5c65-bc37-a382d6255263" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc0bcd5d-4d1d-5e96-9f96-539ff833a355" :question/math-academy-id "q-336358" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
